`[myVar]`
```cpp
int myVar = 5;
auto lambda = [myVar]() { /* myVar is a copy here */ };
```

`[&myRef]`
```cpp
int myRef = 10;
auto lambda = [&myRef]() { /* myRef refers to the original */ };
```

No, `count` cannot be modified inside `f`. When captured by value, variables are `const` by default within the lambda. To enable modification of the *captured copy*, the lambda must be declared `mutable`:
```cpp
int count = 0;
auto f = [count]() mutable {
    count++; // Valid now, modifies the lambda's copy
};
```

A dangling reference occurs when a reference points to an object that no longer exists (e.g., has gone out of scope). When capturing by reference (`[&var]`), if the lambda outlives the `var` it references, the lambda will attempt to access an invalid memory location, leading to undefined behavior.
```cpp
std::function<void()> create_bad_lambda() {
    int temp_val = 42;
    // DANGER: temp_val goes out of scope when function returns
    return [&temp_val]() { std::cout << temp_val << std::endl; };
}
// main()
// auto f = create_bad_lambda();
// f(); // Undefined behavior!
```

Combine them with a comma: `[name, &score]`
```cpp
std::string name = "Alice";
int score = 100;
auto display_info = [name, &score]() {
    std::cout << name << ": " << score << std::endl;
    score += 10; // Modifies original score
};
```
